{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JkiyUqmMVVXZL75zy63jCR","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Token-based splay tree.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! Replaces the C `picosplay_tree_t` (top-down splay with intrusive\n4\t//! `picosplay_node_t`s embedded in the caller's structs and four\n5\t//! function pointers — `comparator`, `create`, `delete_node`,\n6\t//! `node_value` — plumbed through the API).\n7\t//!\n8\t//! ## API shape\n9\t//!\n10\t//! `SplayTree<K, V>` is a slotmap-backed ordered map.  `K: Ord`\n11\t//! provides comparison; `V` is the stored value (typically a\n12\t//! token into another arena).  Callers handle [`SplayToken`]s\n13\t//! returned by `insert`/`find`; the parent/left/right linkage\n14\t//! lives inside the slot, never inside the caller's struct.\n15\t//!\n16\t//! Tokens are stable across operations on *other* keys.  A splay\n17\t//! rotation that lifts node A to the root rewrites the linkage of\n18\t//! the rotation path, but slot indices don't move and tokens for\n19\t//! those nodes stay valid.  Removal bumps the slot's generation\n20\t//! and frees the slot for reuse — old tokens then return `None`\n21\t//! from [`SplayTree::get`].\n22\t//!\n23\t//! ## Phase 4 plan — implementation\n24\t//!\n25\t//! Phase 4: hand-rolled slotmap + splay tree.  One of:\n26\t//!\n27\t//! 1. **Hand-roll the slotmap.**  Internal layout:\n28\t//!\n29\t//!    ```ignore\n30\t//!    struct Slot<K, V> {\n31\t//!        generation: u32,\n32\t//!        state: SlotState<K, V>,\n33\t//!    }\n34\t//!    enum SlotState<K, V> {\n35\t//!        Free   { next_free: Option<u32> },\n36\t//!        Filled {\n37\t//!            key:    K,\n38\t//!            value:  V,\n39\t//!            parent: Option<u32>,\n40\t//!            left:   Option<u32>,\n41\t//!            right:  Option<u32>,\n42\t//!        },\n43\t//!    }\n44\t//!    pub struct SplayTree<K, V> {\n45\t//!        slots: Vec<Slot<K, V>>,\n46\t//!        free:  Option<u32>,\n47\t//!        root:  Option<u32>,\n48\t//!        len:   usize,\n49\t//!    }\n50\t//!    ```\n51\t//!\n52\t//!    Standard top-down splay over `u32` indices.  Every \"follow a\n53\t//!    pointer\" in the C body becomes \"index into `self.slots`.\"\n54\t//!\n55\t//! 2. **Replace with a third-party crate.**  `splay_tree`,\n56\t//!    `splay-tree`, or BTreeMap (if log-N is fine and we don't\n57\t//!    actually need access locality).  picoquic uses splay\n58\t//!    specifically for the access-locality property in\n59\t//!    `connection_wake_tree` (the next-to-fire connection is usually the\n60\t//!    one we just touched).  `BTreeMap` is the safe-default"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"22223bee-eee6-4df4-be23-f562ef79ea05","uuid":"225fe6cf-1506-4c69-8058-a9aa2cd21b2b","timestamp":"2026-05-07T06:23:41.672Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/richbarn/Projects/picoquic/rs/fq/src/splay.rs","content":"//! Token-based splay tree.\n//!\n//! Replaces the C `picosplay_tree_t` (top-down splay with intrusive\n//! `picosplay_node_t`s embedded in the caller's structs and four\n//! function pointers — `comparator`, `create`, `delete_node`,\n//! `node_value` — plumbed through the API).\n//!\n//! ## API shape\n//!\n//! `SplayTree<K, V>` is a slotmap-backed ordered map.  `K: Ord`\n//! provides comparison; `V` is the stored value (typically a\n//! token into another arena).  Callers handle [`SplayToken`]s\n//! returned by `insert`/`find`; the parent/left/right linkage\n//! lives inside the slot, never inside the caller's struct.\n//!\n//! Tokens are stable across operations on *other* keys.  A splay\n//! rotation that lifts node A to the root rewrites the linkage of\n//! the rotation path, but slot indices don't move and tokens for\n//! those nodes stay valid.  Removal bumps the slot's generation\n//! and frees the slot for reuse — old tokens then return `None`\n//! from [`SplayTree::get`].\n//!\n//! ## Phase 4 plan — implementation\n//!\n//! Phase 4: hand-rolled slotmap + splay tree.  One of:\n//!\n//! 1. **Hand-roll the slotmap.**  Internal layout:\n//!\n//!    ```ignore\n//!    struct Slot<K, V> {\n//!        generation: u32,\n//!        state: SlotState<K, V>,\n//!    }\n//!    enum SlotState<K, V> {\n//!        Free   { next_free: Option<u32> },\n//!        Filled {\n//!            key:    K,\n//!            value:  V,\n//!            parent: Option<u32>,\n//!            left:   Option<u32>,\n//!            right:  Option<u32>,\n//!        },\n//!    }\n//!    pub struct SplayTree<K, V> {\n//!        slots: Vec<Slot<K, V>>,\n//!        free:  Option<u32>,\n//!        root:  Option<u32>,\n//!        len:   usize,\n//!    }\n//!    ```\n//!\n//!    Standard top-down splay over `u32` indices.  Every \"follow a\n//!    pointer\" in the C body becomes \"index into `self.slots`.\"\n//!\n//! 2. **Replace with a third-party crate.**  `splay_tree`,\n//!    `splay-tree`, or BTreeMap (if log-N is fine and we don't\n//!    actually need access locality).  picoquic uses splay\n//!    specifically for the access-locality property in\n//!    `connection_wake_tree` (the next-to-fire connection is usually the\n//!    one we just touched).  `BTreeMap` is the safe-default","numLines":60,"startLine":1,"totalLines":645}}}
